🔹 🎯 Task 3: Using super() in Constructor

You are building an employee hierarchy with Person as the base and Employee as the subclass.

📥 Input:
String name;
int age;
String employeeId;
double salary;

⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a Person class with:
  - Protected fields: name, age
  - Constructor Person(String name, int age)
  - Method info() that returns "Person{name='<name>', age=<age>}"
• Create Employee class extending Person with:
  - Private fields: employeeId, salary
  - Constructor Employee(String name, int age, String employeeId, double salary) that calls super(name, age)
  - Override info() to return "Employee{name='<name>', age=<age>, id='<employeeId>', salary=$<salary>}"
• In main(), create two Employee objects and print their info

⚠️ Watch out:
• super() MUST be the first statement in the child constructor. You cannot initialize parent fields directly in the child constructor — you must delegate to super().
• In JS, you'd call super() in a subclass constructor, but Java enforces it — if the parent has a constructor, the child MUST call super().
• Overriding a method completely replaces the parent's version unless you explicitly call super.method() to use both.

📤 Output format:
Employee{name='<name1>', age=<age1>, id='<id1>', salary=$<salary1>}
Employee{name='<name2>', age=<age2>, id='<id2>', salary=$<salary2>}

📌 Test cases:
Input: Employee e1 = new Employee("Alice", 30, "EMP001", 50000.0); Employee e2 = new Employee("Bob", 35, "EMP002", 60000.0);
Output:
Employee{name='Alice', age=30, id='EMP001', salary=$50000.0}
Employee{name='Bob', age=35, id='EMP002', salary=$60000.0}

Input: Employee e1 = new Employee("Charlie", 28, "EMP003", 45000.0); Employee e2 = new Employee("Diana", 32, "EMP004", 55000.0);
Output:
Employee{name='Charlie', age=28, id='EMP003', salary=$45000.0}
Employee{name='Diana', age=32, id='EMP004', salary=$55000.0}

Input: Employee e1 = new Employee("Eve", 29, "EMP005", 52000.0); Employee e2 = new Employee("Frank", 40, "EMP006", 70000.0);
Output:
Employee{name='Eve', age=29, id='EMP005', salary=$52000.0}
Employee{name='Frank', age=40, id='EMP006', salary=$70000.0}

Input: Employee e1 = new Employee("Grace", 26, "EMP007", 43000.0); Employee e2 = new Employee("Henry", 45, "EMP008", 80000.0};
Output:
