🔥 🎯 Bonus Task: Complex Inheritance with Array of Objects

You are building a zoo management system with animals of different types in an array.

📥 Input:
String[] animalNames;
String[] animalTypes; // "Dog" or "Cat"

⚙️ Requirements:
• Create an Animal abstract class (or use inheritance) with:
  - Protected fields: name, type
  - Constructor Animal(String name, String type)
  - Method makeSound() that prints "<name> makes a generic sound"
  - Method getDetails() that returns "Animal{name='<name>', type='<type>'}"
• Create Dog class extending Animal with:
  - Constructor Dog(String name) that calls super(name, "Dog")
  - Override makeSound() to print "<name> barks"
• Create Cat class extending Animal with:
  - Constructor Cat(String name) that calls super(name, "Cat")
  - Override makeSound() to print "<name> meows"
• In main(), create an array of 4 animals (mix of dogs and cats), print each animal's details and sound

⚠️ Watch out:
• You'll store Dog and Cat objects in an Animal[] array. This is polymorphism — each object's makeSound() calls its own override, not the parent's.
• In JS, you'd use duck typing; in Java, you need a common parent class or interface.
• When calling makeSound() on an Animal[] array element, it calls the correct overridden method based on the actual object type, not the array type.
• Array initialization with mixed types: Animal[] animals = new Animal[4]; animals[0] = new Dog("Rex"); animals[1] = new Cat("Whiskers");

📤 Output format:
Animal{name='<name1>', type='<type1>'}
<name1> <sound>
Animal{name='<name2>', type='<type2>'}
<name2> <sound>
(repeated for each animal)

